1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,687
Sam: I switched the site to dark mode, refreshed, and it went right back to light. Why?

2
00:00:06,037 --> 00:00:13,152
Byte: Because the page forgot. Every refresh starts from scratch, unless the app saves your choice in the browser.

3
00:00:14,602 --> 00:00:22,040
Byte: The simplest place is localStorage. One call saves the theme: setItem, with a key and a value.

4
00:00:22,990 --> 00:00:27,771
Byte: Close the browser, come back next week, and getItem still returns dark.

5
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:35,927
Byte: Every tab on the same site shares it. Change it in one tab, and the other tabs get a storage event.

6
00:00:36,878 --> 00:00:42,745
Byte: But it only stores strings. Save the number forty two, and you get back the string forty two.

7
00:00:43,495 --> 00:00:53,272
Byte: Save an object, and you get object Object. So use JSON.stringify going in, and JSON.parse coming out.

8
00:00:54,222 --> 00:01:01,064
Byte: The limit is about five megabytes per site. Our test hit a quota error near five million characters.

9
00:01:01,814 --> 00:01:07,815
Byte: And it is synchronous, so every read and write blocks the page for a moment. Keep it small.

10
00:01:09,564 --> 00:01:14,150
Sam: What if I only need data while the tab is open? Like a half filled checkout form?

11
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:20,422
Byte: That is sessionStorage. Same methods, but every tab gets its own private copy.

12
00:01:21,272 --> 00:01:30,143
Byte: Reload the page, and the draft is still there. Open the site in a new tab, and that tab starts empty. Close the tab, and it is gone.

13
00:01:31,893 --> 00:01:34,050
Sam: And cookies? They are the oldest one.

14
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:40,511
Byte: A cookie is a small string the browser sends back to the server, with every request to that site.

15
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:47,537
Byte: That is the whole point. The server can read it, which is how it remembers that you are logged in.

16
00:01:48,388 --> 00:01:55,939
Byte: The server sets one with a Set-Cookie header. In our test, the next request carried it back automatically.

17
00:01:56,788 --> 00:02:05,571
Byte: Mark it HttpOnly, and scripts cannot read it. document.cookie showed the theme, not the session id.

18
00:02:06,521 --> 00:02:14,646
Byte: Add Secure, so it only travels over HTTPS, and SameSite, to limit when other sites can send it.

19
00:02:15,496 --> 00:02:22,908
Byte: But cookies are tiny: about four kilobytes each. Our five thousand character cookie was silently dropped.

20
00:02:24,658 --> 00:02:29,315
Sam: What if my app needs lots of data, like a product catalog that works offline?

21
00:02:29,665 --> 00:02:34,949
Byte: Then use IndexedDB. It is a real database, inside the browser.

22
00:02:35,799 --> 00:02:44,827
Byte: It stores real objects, not just strings. Our saved order came back with a real date, a real array, and even a file blob.

23
00:02:45,777 --> 00:02:52,993
Byte: Writes happen in transactions, so they fully succeed, or not at all. And indexes make lookups fast.

24
00:02:53,843 --> 00:03:01,758
Byte: It is asynchronous. The put call returns right away, and the result arrives later, so the page never freezes.

25
00:03:02,608 --> 00:03:08,947
Byte: And it holds far more than local storage, often gigabytes, depending on free disk space.

26
00:03:10,697 --> 00:03:18,439
Byte: For offline pages, there is the Cache API. It saves whole requests, together with their responses.

27
00:03:19,289 --> 00:03:26,254
Byte: A service worker answers from that cache when the network is down. That is how an app still opens offline.

28
00:03:28,004 --> 00:03:35,940
Byte: A few rules apply to all of them. First, data belongs to one origin: the scheme, the host, and the port.

29
00:03:36,891 --> 00:03:45,772
Byte: Second, the browser may clear it when the disk runs low. navigator.storage.persist asks it to keep yours.

30
00:03:46,721 --> 00:03:56,177
Byte: Third, any script on your page can read local storage, session storage, and IndexedDB. So never keep tokens or secrets there.

31
00:03:58,128 --> 00:03:59,886
Sam: So which one should I pick?

32
00:04:00,235 --> 00:04:07,104
Byte: The login session: an HttpOnly cookie. The theme or language: local storage.

33
00:04:07,855 --> 00:04:17,503
Byte: A form draft for one tab: session storage. Big or offline data: IndexedDB. Offline pages: the Cache API.

34
00:04:18,453 --> 00:04:21,643
Sam: Got it. Same browser, different shelves for different things.
